# systemd timer: hourly SEP-1 issuer-metadata refresh.
#
# Rendered by Ansible. Companion to sep1-refresh.service. This is the cron
# that previously never existed — issuer org_name / org_verified only updated
# on a manual run, so the /v1/issuers metadata went stale.
#
# Time slot: :12 past every hour + up to 5 min jitter. It was 05:12 UTC once
# a day, between supply-snapshot (04:42) and compute-completeness (05:30);
# the minute is kept so the daily-slot reasoning still holds for the 05:12
# tick. Persistent=true so a reboot doesn't skip a tick.
#
# WHY HOURLY. At 500 attempts a day against 76,658 domains a full cycle took
# 153 days, so the job's own `-older-than 24h` was unreachable by two orders
# of magnitude. The arithmetic for 750/hour is in sep1-refresh.service.j2;
# the short version is that 24 small runs deliver the same daily throughput
# as one big one, with a 25% duty cycle, a 25-minute worst case instead of a
# six-hour one, and an hour of lost progress instead of a day when a run
# fails.
#
# ON OVERLAP. A run takes ~15 min, so the :12 tick can still be going when
# galexie-archive-fill starts at :17, and the 05:12 and 06:12 ticks overlap
# compute-completeness (05:30) and verify-served-values (06:20). That is
# accepted rather than overlooked: this job is network-bound, and its entire
# Postgres footprint per run is one indexed `SELECT … LIMIT 750` plus at most
# 750 single-row UPDATEs on a plain table. It is not one of the heavy jobs
# the morning stagger exists to keep apart.

[Unit]
Description=Hourly SEP-1 issuer-metadata refresh
Documentation=https://github.com/Stellar-Index/StellarIndex/blob/main/internal/metadata/doc.go

[Timer]
OnCalendar=*-*-* *:12:00 UTC
RandomizedDelaySec=300
Persistent=true
Unit=sep1-refresh.service

[Install]
WantedBy=timers.target
